---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
import manifest from '@busy-office/ui/behaviors-manifest';
import eventsManifest from '@busy-office/ui/events';
import keymapManifest from '@busy-office/ui/keymap';
import api from '@busy-office/ui/api';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
// escape first, THEN backticks→<code> — event docs mention literal tags
// (`<tr>`, `<input>`) that must never reach set:html raw
const fmt = (s: string) =>
  s.replace(/&/g, '&amp;').replace(/</g, '&lt;').replace(/>/g, '&gt;')
   .replace(/`([^`]+)`/g, '<code>$1</code>');
const refresh = `// Bound table containers re-derive selection state automatically when an
// htmx:after:swap bubbles through them. Any OTHER swap mechanism calls:
import { refreshDataTable } from "@busy-office/ui/js";
refreshDataTable(container);

// Only NEW containers swapped in later need re-binding:
document.body.addEventListener("htmx:after:swap", (e) => initDataTables(e.target));`;
const inits = Object.entries(manifest.behaviors)
  .filter(([name]) => name.startsWith('init'))
  .map(([name, b]: [string, any]) => [name, b.summary, b.hooks]);
---
<Gallery title="JS behaviors" description="The framework is CSS-first, not CSS-only. Small zero-dependency behaviors cover what the platform cannot do, each a call-once init using event delegation.">
  <p class="demo-note">
    The framework is CSS-first, not CSS-only. {inits.length} small behaviors
    (zero dependencies) cover what the platform can't do or what
    accessibility demands: each is a <strong>call-once init</strong> using
    document-level event delegation, so DOM swaps (HTMX, Alpine, anything)
    never require re-binding.
  </p>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The inits <span class="bo-badge">generated</span></h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Init</th><th scope="col">What it adds</th><th scope="col">DOM hooks it reads</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {inits.map(([name, summary, hooks]: [string, string, string[]]) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="bo-data-table__col--code">{name}()</td>
              <td>{summary}{summary.endsWith('…') ? '' : '.'}</td>
              <td class="bo-u-text-muted">{hooks.map((h) => (h.startsWith('data-') ? h : `.${h}`)).join(', ')}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Keyboard support <span class="bo-badge">generated</span></h2>
    <p>The four behaviors that own their own key handling — everything else
    is native (a plain <code>&lt;input&gt;</code>, a native <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>'s
    own focus trap) or has no keyboard surface (delegation-only behaviors like
    alerts). Extracted from source, so it can't drift from what's actually
    wired.</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Behavior</th><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">Does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {keymapManifest.behaviors.flatMap((b: any) => b.keys.map((k: any, i: number) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="bo-data-table__col--code">{i === 0 ? `${b.name}()` : ''}</td>
              <td class="bo-data-table__col--code">{k.key}</td>
              <td set:html={fmt(k.does)} />
            </tr>
          )))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The delegation model</h2>
    <p>Each init installs one document-level listener and resolves its target at
    event time; element-level listeners (a dialog's close handler) bind lazily on
    first use, tracked in a <code>WeakSet</code> — never serialized into the DOM.
    What you can rely on:</p>
    <ul class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li>Triggers may render before their dialog exists.</li>
      <li>Swapped-in content works without re-init.</li>
      <li>Clone / morph swaps can't strand stale state.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Event wiring is swap-proof; derived state is re-computed.</strong> The
    one HTMX-aware line in core JS is the <code>htmx:after:swap</code> event
    <em>name</em> a table container listens for:</p>
    <pre><code>{refresh}</code></pre>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Intent events</h2>
    <p>Behaviors report user intent through DOM events — your code
    decides what to persist. The full generated list (name, target,
    when it fires, payload) is the
    <a href={base + '/reference/events'}>intent-event index</a>; it is
    look-up material, not something to read once.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>State attributes <span class="bo-badge">generated</span></h2>
    <p>Two attributes, deliberately kept separate rather than merged into
    one lifecycle vocabulary — a component is either OPEN or BUSY, and
    conflating the two would make "is this dialog open while its save is
    in flight" unrepresentable in one value.</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Attribute</th><th scope="col">Values</th><th scope="col">Means</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--code">data-state</td>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--code">{api.dataAttrValues['data-state'].join('|')}</td>
            <td>structural: is this open or closed (dialog, offcanvas,
            dashboard cards, the motion module's collapse), or where a
            multi-step control is in its own sequence (stepper,
            approval-workflow's timeline). Same attribute name, two
            genuinely different domains — read it per component, never
            assume the full value set applies everywhere it appears.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--code">data-loading</td>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--code">{api.dataAttrValues['data-loading'].join('|')}</td>
            <td>busy: an async request is in flight (button, data-table).
            The opt-in <code>@busy-office/ui/css/htmx</code> module bridges
            it onto HTMX's own request state — <code>.htmx-request
            [data-loading]</code> disables pointer events for the
            duration with no JS wiring on your part.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Where a component's states don't fit either
    shape, it gets its own attribute rather than overloading these two:
    <code>data-row-state</code> ({api.dataAttrValues['data-row-state'].join('|')})
    on data-table rows, <code>data-day</code>
    ({api.dataAttrValues['data-day'].join('|')}) on the calendar. A shared
    name across unrelated domains is a worse contract than two honestly
    different ones.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The Save sequence</h2>
    <p>The pattern behind every Save/Submit/Approve button in this
    framework's pattern catalogue, as it actually composes today — no
    dedicated "loading state" component, because the pieces above already
    cover it:</p>
    <ol class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li><strong>Idle</strong> — the button reads "Save".</li>
      <li><strong>Saving</strong> — your JS (or the HTMX bridge, for free)
      sets <code>data-loading="true"</code> and <code>aria-busy="true"</code>
      on the button, and swaps its text to "Saving…". Two channels: the
      text change is what a sighted user reads; <code>aria-busy</code> is
      what a screen reader announces. Neither alone is the contract.</li>
      <li><strong>Saved (or Error)</strong> — clear both attributes and
      restore the button's resting label, or route to your error state
      (a field's <code>aria-invalid</code>, a summary, a toast — pattern-
      specific, not this button's job).</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>Deliberately no spinner icon or
    success checkmark on the button itself</strong> — a real, measured
    decision, not an oversight: an opacity-dimmed solid-fill button drops
    to roughly 3.24:1 text contrast (axe color-contrast), under the 4.5:1
    minimum. The button's own visible channel stays the text swap plus
    <code>cursor: progress</code>; a spinner or checkmark belongs beside
    or inside the button as your own markup if the pattern wants one —
    the framework does not force a specific glyph onto every Save action.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Import paths</h2>
    <pre><code>import {'{ initDialogs }'} from "@busy-office/ui/js";          // barrel
import {'{ initDialogs }'} from "@busy-office/ui/js/behaviors/dialog.js"; // granular</code></pre>
  </section>
  <Related links={[["/getting-started/installation", "Installation"], ["/getting-started/troubleshooting", "Troubleshooting"], ["/base/motion", "Motion (intent vocabulary, entrance/exit classes)"]]} />
</Gallery>
